#%PAM-1.0
#
# The stack the lockscreen authenticates against.
#
# pshell ships this rather than using /etc/pam.d/login, which is what
# hyprlock and swaylock include. The system stack is written for a login
# that runs as root: it carries pam_nologin, pam_systemd_home and two
# `required` calls to pam_faillock, and every one of those is a way for an
# unprivileged screen locker to be refused a password that is correct.
#
# Nothing here uses `include`. That is deliberate and load-bearing: PAM
# resolves an include against the directory the stack was started from, so
# a stack that included anything could not live outside /etc/pam.d, which
# is where a shell you clone into ~/.config cannot write.
#
# Copied in shape from DankMaterialShell's assets/pam/login, which solves
# the same problem the same way.
#
# The trade: no pam_faillock means no rate limit on guesses at the lock
# screen. If you want one, install a stack of your own at
# /etc/pam.d/pshell -- it is preferred over this file whenever it exists --
# or point `lock.pamService` at a service you already trust.

auth      required     pam_env.so
auth      sufficient   pam_unix.so       try_first_pass nullok
auth      required     pam_deny.so

account   required     pam_unix.so
password  required     pam_deny.so
session   required     pam_permit.so
